1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:03,000
Welcome to the Metamodel Architecture
Demonstrator.

2
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:10,660
Chapter 1: Lost in the Forest - Why 
Instance Graphs Are Not Enough.

3
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:16,059
Look at the graph currently on our canvas.

4
00:00:18,320 --> 00:00:23,300
In our previous scenarios, we tracked a
physical suspension arm, serial number

5
00:00:23,300 --> 00:00:29,360
ARM_SN_404, across its operational
lifecycle.

6
00:00:30,920 --> 00:00:36,980
We watched it move from a warehouse into a
vehicle slot, develop a crack, and return to storage.

7
00:00:39,560 --> 00:00:44,560
For a database programmer or a field
engineer, this instance graph is fascinating.

8
00:00:46,680 --> 00:00:50,302
Wait long enough, however, and a real
automotive program 

9
00:00:50,302 --> 00:00:52,420
will not have 10 nodes on this screen.

10
00:00:54,580 --> 00:00:59,680
It will have 10 million nodes, spanning
mechanical parts, electronic control

11
00:00:59,680 --> 00:01:02,800
units, software allocations, and safety
hazards.

12
00:01:05,680 --> 00:01:08,272
As Robert Nilsson observes in his paper,


13
00:01:08,272 --> 00:01:11,380
"The Knowledge System: Is It One Ring to
Rule Them All?"

14
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:16,260
Engineers quickly hit a wall where they
cannot see the forest for all the trees.

15
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:23,680
Worse still, enterprise architects are
forced to ask two uncomfortable questions.

16
00:01:23,680 --> 00:01:30,000
First, is it even relevant to talk about
a single forest at all?

17
00:01:32,160 --> 00:01:36,660
And second, are there different kinds of
forests depending on who is looking?

18
00:01:39,900 --> 00:01:45,100
To answer those questions, we must step
into the architectural elevator and ride

19
00:01:45,100 --> 00:01:47,400
up from the factory floor to the meta
level.

20
00:01:49,820 --> 00:01:56,140
Watch the canvas as we switch our
perspective to the IEC 63339 meta model view.

21
00:01:59,300 --> 00:02:04,140
Instead of looking only at individual nuts
and bolts, we are now looking at a

22
00:02:04,140 --> 00:02:07,860
four-story architectural building inside a
single graph.

23
00:02:11,180 --> 00:02:17,060
Chapter 2: Riding the Four-Story 
Elevator of IEC 63339.

24
00:02:20,140 --> 00:02:22,939
Let us walk through the four floors of
this building 

25
00:02:22,939 --> 00:02:25,140
using the color legend on the bottom left.

26
00:02:27,420 --> 00:02:34,400
The top floor is represented by the bright
cyan boxes, the URMSM layer or Unified

27
00:02:34,400 --> 00:02:36,620
Reference Model for Smart Manufacturing.

28
00:02:39,160 --> 00:02:41,800
This is where organizational thinking
begins.

29
00:02:44,040 --> 00:02:46,980
Notice the three high-level dimensions on
the canvas:

30
00:02:47,620 --> 00:02:52,040
product development, manufacturing, and
standardized approach.

31
00:02:54,480 --> 00:02:59,660
Each dimension is broken down into
concrete aspects or stakeholder viewpoints

32
00:02:59,660 --> 00:03:06,340
as defined in ISO 42010, such as bill of
materials, concept and

33
00:03:06,340 --> 00:03:09,600
functional context, and top-level
ontology.

34
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:17,100
An aspect does not store car parts. It
defines what a specific engineering

35
00:03:17,100 --> 00:03:18,980
department actually cares about.

36
00:03:21,920 --> 00:03:24,158
Now, follow the aggregation arrows 

37
00:03:24,158 --> 00:03:27,920
from those cyan aspects to the next floor, 
the gray boxes.

38
00:03:30,300 --> 00:03:35,100
This is the SMRM layer, the Smart
Manufacturing Reference Model.

39
00:03:37,680 --> 00:03:44,140
Here live our canonical domain concepts:
system, physical part, functional slot,

40
00:03:44,140 --> 00:03:46,100
and product configuration element.

41
00:03:48,580 --> 00:03:53,760
Think of these gray boxes as your
enterprise SysML or UML concept dictionary.

42
00:03:55,960 --> 00:04:00,780
This is where human engineers across
different departments agree on what words mean.

43
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:10,140
Next, follow the specialization arrows to
the third floor, the white boxes with dark borders.

44
00:04:12,820 --> 00:04:17,959
This is the SMIM layer, the Smart
Manufacturing Implementation Model.

45
00:04:20,440 --> 00:04:25,320
A gray concept like system or part is not
yet a database rule.

46
00:04:27,620 --> 00:04:33,420
At the SMIM layer, we commit to formal
ontological classes and constraints, here

47
00:04:33,420 --> 00:04:41,140
shown as BFO three-dimensional continuance, 
material entity, BFO site, and quality.

48
00:04:44,040 --> 00:04:48,140
Finally, follow the dashed instantiation 
lines to the ground floor,

49
00:04:48,740 --> 00:04:51,780
the SMS layer or real data in the tool.

50
00:04:54,200 --> 00:05:02,240
Look at vehicle_1, ARM_SN_404, and its
recorded cracks.

51
00:05:04,660 --> 00:05:09,640
In fact, watch what happens if we use the
engineer's toolbar right now to register 

52
00:05:09,640 --> 00:05:17,000
a new component, ECU_MODULE_7, and install
it into vehicle_1.

53
00:05:19,820 --> 00:05:24,640
The new instance immediately appears on
the canvas and wires itself to material

54
00:05:24,640 --> 00:05:27,300
entity via a dashed instantiation link.

55
00:05:29,640 --> 00:05:34,780
Abstract theory at the top is directly connected 
to live engineering data at the bottom.

56
00:05:38,540 --> 00:05:44,100
Chapter 3:Formalizing Scope - Model 
What You Need, Not What You Can.

57
00:05:47,400 --> 00:05:52,540
Now, let us use this four-layer machine to
solve the first major dilemma highlighted

58
00:05:52,540 --> 00:05:54,860
in the paper that inspired this
demonstrator:

59
00:05:55,520 --> 00:05:59,220
How do we stop ontologists from
over-engineering the meta model?

60
00:06:02,088 --> 00:06:04,908
In systems engineering, there is a golden
rule:

61
00:06:05,568 --> 00:06:08,168
model what you need, not what you can.

62
00:06:11,068 --> 00:06:15,728
Every gray concept box we add to our
repository increases the number of rules

63
00:06:15,728 --> 00:06:17,728
we must maintain across the enterprise.

64
00:06:20,108 --> 00:06:27,948
Look at the gray box labeled Concept,
Functional Object, or Slot based on ISO 81346.

65
00:06:30,688 --> 00:06:33,328
Why does our database have functional
slots at all?

66
00:06:35,548 --> 00:06:40,888
Because the cyan aspect, Concept and
Functional Context, is currently active in

67
00:06:40,888 --> 00:06:44,388
our toolbar, explicitly demanding that
concept.

68
00:06:47,288 --> 00:06:51,620
Watch what happens when an organization
decides that for a simpler project, 

69
00:06:51,620 --> 00:06:54,117
they do not need functional slot analysis. 

70
00:06:54,117 --> 00:06:57,548
They only care about which 
physical part is in which vehicle.

71
00:06:59,888 --> 00:07:04,728
Let us uncheck the Functional Context
aspect toggle in the Meta-Model toolbar.

72
00:07:07,988 --> 00:07:09,568
Look closely at the canvas.

73
00:07:10,228 --> 00:07:13,808
Three things happen simultaneously across
all four floors.

74
00:07:16,468 --> 00:07:21,047
First, at the gray SMRM layer, the
functional slot concept 

75
00:07:21,047 --> 00:07:24,029
vanishes completely, 
and the aspect links via 

76
00:07:24,029 --> 00:07:27,348
adirect scope arrow straight 
to the system concept.

77
00:07:30,488 --> 00:07:32,986
Second, at the white SMIM layer, 

78
00:07:32,986 --> 00:07:37,148
BFO site is disconnected from 
the domain concept model.

79
00:07:39,748 --> 00:07:45,288
And third, most importantly, look at the
live instance data on the canvas and in

80
00:07:45,288 --> 00:07:46,888
the node store table on the right.

81
00:07:49,188 --> 00:07:57,188
The instance node 
slot_vehicle_1_ARM_SN_404

82
00:07:57,188 --> 00:07:59,968
has been completely pruned from the
database.

83
00:08:02,468 --> 00:08:05,488
The node store drops from six nodes down
to five.

84
00:08:08,748 --> 00:08:12,287
Even if we switch to the logical SMIM view
for a moment,

85
00:08:12,888 --> 00:08:19,868
we see that vehicle_1 and ARM_SN_404 now
connect

86
00:08:19,868 --> 00:08:22,408
directly without an intermediate slot.

87
00:08:25,288 --> 00:08:29,748
By tracing concepts strictly to
stakeholder aspects, the architecture

88
00:08:29,748 --> 00:08:34,648
proves mathematically which concepts are
necessary and which ones are dead weight.

89
00:08:37,088 --> 00:08:39,335
Let us return to the Meta-Model view 

90
00:08:39,335 --> 00:08:43,168
and re-enable the Functional Context 
aspect to bring our slot back.

91
00:08:46,468 --> 00:08:51,748
Chapter 4: The Red Arrows - Deciding 
What is a Configuration Element.

92
00:08:54,928 --> 00:08:58,608
Let us move to the second architectural
dilemma explored in the paper:

93
00:08:59,348 --> 00:09:02,668
forming detailed rules for product
configuration management.

94
00:09:05,468 --> 00:09:07,528
When you release a product configuration,

95
00:09:08,168 --> 00:09:11,068
what exactly is a product configuration
element?

96
00:09:13,528 --> 00:09:18,468
Is it only the physical hardware box, or
is it the functional allocation and

97
00:09:18,468 --> 00:09:19,908
software environment as well?

98
00:09:22,348 --> 00:09:27,328
Follow the red specialization arrows at
the bottom of the gray SMRM layer.

99
00:09:30,448 --> 00:09:34,988
Right now, let us set the configuration
rule selector on the right of our meta

100
00:09:34,988 --> 00:09:36,688
toolbar to Part Only.

101
00:09:39,588 --> 00:09:44,028
Notice that only a single red
specialization arrow points from Physical

102
00:09:44,028 --> 00:09:46,388
Part to Product Configuration Element.

103
00:09:48,708 --> 00:09:51,648
This represents a traditional
hardware-centric view.

104
00:09:52,248 --> 00:09:55,308
Only physical parts carry configuration
part numbers.

105
00:09:58,208 --> 00:10:03,588
Now, watch the bottom of the graph as we
change the rule to Part plus Slot Allocation.

106
00:10:06,768 --> 00:10:11,448
A second red specialization arrow
immediately appears, connecting Functional

107
00:10:11,448 --> 00:10:13,908
Slot to Product Configuration Element.

108
00:10:16,248 --> 00:10:20,688
In this regime, both the physical
component and its functional allocation

109
00:10:20,688 --> 00:10:23,428
slot are tracked under configuration
control.

110
00:10:26,348 --> 00:10:32,188
And if we switch the rule to System plus
Part plus Slot, a third red arrow locks

111
00:10:32,188 --> 00:10:35,488
the top-level system into configuration
management as well.

112
00:10:38,408 --> 00:10:42,828
Notice how these critical engineering
governance decisions are made cleanly at

113
00:10:42,828 --> 00:10:48,048
the gray SMRM concept layer, completely
independent of whether our underlying

114
00:10:48,048 --> 00:10:51,588
database is Neo4j or an RDF triple store.

115
00:10:54,968 --> 00:11:00,128
Chapter 5: The Firewall Against Technology
Churn - Swapping Standards.

116
00:11:03,308 --> 00:11:07,408
This brings us to the ultimate architectural 
question in the automotive industry.

117
00:11:08,188 --> 00:11:12,108
In a four-year vehicle program where
requirements evolve constantly,

118
00:11:12,748 --> 00:11:18,128
how do we avoid being paralyzed by a rigid
ontology or a painful database migration?

119
00:11:18,128 --> 00:11:24,728
The answer is architectural decoupling
across layers.

120
00:11:27,408 --> 00:11:32,148
Watch all four floors of our metagraph as
we switch the top-level ontology at the

121
00:11:32,148 --> 00:11:38,848
top of the screen from BFO in 3D to ISO
15926 in 4D.

122
00:11:41,708 --> 00:11:45,788
Look closely at what changed on the canvas
and what did not change.

123
00:11:48,488 --> 00:11:53,308
The cyan URMSM dimensions and aspects did
not change.

124
00:11:55,768 --> 00:12:02,128
The gray SMRM domain concepts, System,
Slot, Part, and Product Configuration

125
00:12:02,128 --> 00:12:06,448
Element, and their red configuration rules
did not change at all.

126
00:12:09,364 --> 00:12:15,624
Only the third floor, the white SMIM
information elements, swapped from BFO

127
00:12:15,624 --> 00:12:22,484
continuants to ISO 15926 four-dimensional
extents, actual individual,

128
00:12:22,484 --> 00:12:29,264
state, and defect class, automatically rewiring 
the live instance data connected to them.

129
00:12:32,144 --> 00:12:38,144
Even if we drop down to a naive graph, the
white SMIM boxes simplify into basic

130
00:12:38,144 --> 00:12:43,264
property graph nodes and inline
attributes, while our gray SMRM concept

131
00:12:43,264 --> 00:12:44,964
dictionary remains untouched.

132
00:12:47,784 --> 00:12:49,544
Let us switch back to BFO.

133
00:12:52,044 --> 00:12:55,524
The SMRM layer acts as a protective
firewall.

134
00:12:56,144 --> 00:13:01,164
Your systems engineers work with stable
domain concepts, while data architects can

135
00:13:01,164 --> 00:13:05,244
evolve or swap the formal ontology and
storage engine underneath.

136
00:13:08,544 --> 00:13:13,644
Chapter 6, closing the loop, from meta model 
to the engineer's desk.

137
00:13:16,944 --> 00:13:23,604
Finally, let us close the loop of IEC
63339 by switching from the meta model

138
00:13:23,604 --> 00:13:26,884
view back to the engineer SMRM viewpoint.

139
00:13:29,744 --> 00:13:35,244
Look at the clean three-node diagram on
the canvas and the hierarchical BOM tree

140
00:13:35,244 --> 00:13:36,864
structure in the top right panel.

141
00:13:39,204 --> 00:13:44,484
The manufacturing engineer looking at this
BOM tree does not see cyan dimensions,

142
00:13:45,004 --> 00:13:49,264
white BFO classes, or RDF reification
hexagons.

143
00:13:51,644 --> 00:13:56,604
Yet every single node they see is
governed, justified, and traced through

144
00:13:56,604 --> 00:13:58,184
that four-layer architecture.

145
00:14:01,084 --> 00:14:07,824
That is why a knowledge system built on
IEC 63339 is not a rigid prison that

146
00:14:07,824 --> 00:14:10,284
forces you to guess every rule on day one.

147
00:14:12,664 --> 00:14:17,644
It is a living map that lets you start
small, justify every concept through a

148
00:14:17,644 --> 00:14:23,044
clear stakeholder aspect, and evolve your
engineering models iteratively without

149
00:14:23,044 --> 00:14:25,444
ever losing sight of the forest for the
trees.
